--- body ---
## Context

The author runs coding agents all day; the 81s full `make check` is paid once
per pre-PR gate and once in CI. `pytest-xdist` is the only remaining lever
after the 1010 re-tiering (check-fast 2.9s, lint 2.1s, both at the pytest
boot+collection floor). This audit answers: is the suite worker-safe, and what
does parallelism actually buy?

## Static census (source-level, all three axes)

| Axis | Finding |
|---|---|
| Environment | Clean by construction — the 0940/0359 hermeticity discipline (explicit `env=` dicts, `child_env()`, no ambient mutation). `os.environ[...]` hits in tests are reads (PATH composition) only |
| Filesystem | Clean. 21/21 shell suites use `mktemp -d`; the Python side uses `tmp_path`. Fixed-path hits are read-only globs (`test_on_end_hook.py:306` inspects the live `/tmp/claude-<uid>` root without writing) or never-executed translation strings (`/tmp/pwn` in `test_hook_launchers.py`) |
| Git state | Clean. No `git config --global` anywhere in tests/ or scripts. All git operations run in per-test tmp fixtures; the two heaviest suites (`test_erg_pr_merge.sh`, `test_seat_runner.sh`) are `mktemp`+trap isolated with env-var-pointed capture files under `$WORK` |

No session-scoped mutating fixtures (one module-scoped read-only census fixture,
per-worker under xdist). No fixed ports. No shared fixed paths written.

## Live trials (venv with pytest-xdist 3.8.0 + system pytest 9.0.3, 24-core host)

| Run | Result | Wall |
|---|---|---|
| Serial baseline | 1263 passed, 2 skipped | 81.0s |
| `-n 4` | 1263 passed, 2 skipped | 38.6s (2.1x) |
| `-n 8` | 1263 passed, 2 skipped | 37.2s (2.2x) |
| `-n 8` repeat | 1263 passed, 2 skipped | 37.4s |

Three parallel executions, zero collisions, zero count drift, no flaky
failures. Scaling plateaus at ~37s: the critical path is the three shell
monoliths, which parallelism cannot touch — `test_erg_pr_merge.sh` (12.2s),
`test_seat_runner.sh` (9.6s), `test_sync_local_main.sh` (3.7s) are single
sequential `.sh` files each driven as one parametrized case.

## Recommendation

Adopt: add `pytest-xdist` to `requirements-dev.txt` (pinned, the same
range-pinning discipline as ruff in 0470) and wire `check-tests` to
`python3 -m pytest tests/ -n 4`. Expected: pre-PR gate 81s -> ~39s, CI
pytest-guard 1m26s -> ~50s. The audit found no hazard that requires a
conservative default; if the author prefers caution, land the dependency and
keep `make check` serial with a `check-par` opt-in target first, flipping the
default after a week of clean parallel runs.

Floor note: going below ~37s requires splitting the three shell monoliths
into independently schedulable cases — a separate ticket, worth it only if
CI latency ever matters beyond that.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Census recorded (this ticket) with all three axes covered
- [ ] Live trials recorded with exact pass/skip counts matching serial
- [ ] Adoption decision recorded by the author: wire -n 4 into check-tests, or dependency + opt-in target
